## The Matte-and-Gloss Combo Everyone's Copying

The matte-and-gloss combo is a manicure where every nail is finished in matte top coat except one, usually the ring finger, which is left glossy. It has become 2026's most-requested technique specifically because it solves a problem full matte sets can create: on their own, ten matte nails can occasionally read a little flat under certain lighting. One glossy nail breaks that up, catches light, and gives the whole set visual depth without changing the colour or the design underneath.

The technique works on any base colour, which is part of why it has spread so fast. Matte black with one glossy black nail. Matte nude with a glossy accent. Even the bold jewel tones above take the combo well, since the single glossy nail acts almost like a highlight in a painting, drawing the eye to one focal point rather than spreading attention evenly across all ten.

Placement matters more than most tutorials mention. The ring finger accent has become the default because it echoes where a statement ring would sit, but a single glossy thumb reads slightly more editorial, and glossy pinky nails on both hands creates a subtler, more symmetrical version of the same effect for anyone who wants the technique without it being the first thing someone notices.

The matte-and-gloss combo is straightforward enough to do at home once the base set is finished. Apply matte top coat to every nail except the one chosen for the accent, let it fully set, then apply a standard glossy top coat to the remaining nail. No special products beyond a matte top coat and a glossy one are needed, which is a large part of why the technique has spread so quickly outside of salons. The only real risk is getting matte top coat on the accent nail by accident, since a stray drop is difficult to correct without redoing that nail from the colour coat up.

